    QSplitter(Qt::Horizontal) inside RoadmapDialog puts a QListWidget
    (objectName "roadmap-toc") on the left and the rendered viewer on
    the right. The list is rebuilt from the markdown on every refresh
    — including live-update events — and entries are indented two
    spaces per level above 1 with level-1 headings shown bold.
    Click/activate an entry → the viewer scrolls to the heading.
  * Added: anchor-emitting renderer. RoadmapDialog::renderHtml now
    prepends <a name="roadmap-toc-N"></a> before each <h1>/<h2>/<h3>/
    <h4> it emits. The N-th anchor matches the N-th entry returned by
    the new pure helper RoadmapDialog::extractToc, which returns a
    QVector<TocEntry> of {level, text, anchor} triples. Both helpers
    are pure and static so they're driven by the feature-conformance
    test without spinning a Qt widget tree.
  * Added: QTextBrowser in place of QTextEdit. Required for
    scrollToAnchor support. Links are pinned non-navigable
    (setOpenLinks(false) / setOpenExternalLinks(false)) so a stray
    markdown link can't replace the document.
  * Fixed: Close button in the Roadmap viewer is now actually
    clickable. The dialog used the role-based
    QDialogButtonBox::rejected signal alone; under some Qt 6 builds
    the standard Close button does not trigger that signal, leaving
    the button looking active but doing nothing. The Close button is
    now wired both via rejected() (belt-and-braces) and via a direct
    clicked connection on the underlying QPushButton retrieved with
    button(QDialogButtonBox::Close). The direct connect is the
    authoritative fix.
  * Tests: tests/features/roadmap_viewer/test_roadmap_viewer.cpp
    extended from 10 to 14 invariants — INV-11 (extractToc returns 5
    entries with matching levels/text/anchors against a known
    multi-level input), INV-12 (renderHtml emits an
    <a name="roadmap-toc-0"> immediately before the first <h2>),
    INV-13 (dialog source-grep: QSplitter + roadmap-toc objectName +
    scrollToAnchor invocation), INV-14 (Close button directly wired
    via QAbstractButton::clicked).

